  "notes": "The same entity as Home Assistant 2026.10.4 sends it with unit_system us_customary: smhi.json's values fed to Home Assistant's WeatherEntity as native values in the units its attributes state (°C, hPa, km/h, km, mm), state and forecasts read back through the weather component with hass.config.units set to US_CUSTOMARY_SYSTEM. Temperatures come back as whole °F (ints: outside °C the weather entity rounds to whole degrees), pressure in inHg, wind in mph, visibility in mi and precipitation in in, each to 2 decimals; humidity, bearings, conditions, datetimes and every other field are as in the metric file. The metric file's notes follow; their numbers are metric. State attributes, daily and twice_daily are core snapshots (state 'cloudy' is asserted by the same test). Core snapshots only entries 0 and 6 of the 60-entry hourly forecast, so hourly is built from the same fixture (smhi.json) through pysmhi's parsing and the weather entity's code, and matches both snapshotted entries. The test does not freeze the clock, so now is the first hourly entry. Every hourly entry carries templow equal to temperature. Daily entries sit at 12:00 UTC; twice-daily ones at 00:00 UTC (night, is_daytime false) and 12:00 UTC (day); the first twice-daily entry is the night half that began at 00:00 UTC today, eleven hours before now. No forecast carries precipitation_probability. Sunny hours after sunset become clear-night. thunder_probability is an extra state attribute. A forecast type comes back null when the library returns fewer than three entries for it. No changes were needed for realism.",
